#### Asymptotic Freedom

Similar to QED, in QCD the renormalization coupling constant, \(\alpha_{\mathrm{s}}\left(\mu\right)\), depends on the renormalization scale. In QED, due to charge screening from vacuum-fluctuation virtual-particles, the coupling, \(\alpha\), decreases with distance. This is known as the running coupling constant in QED. In QCD something similar happens, but gluon self interaction changes the behavior of \(\alpha_{\mathrm{s}}\left(\mu\right)\). The coupling constant \(\alpha_{\mathrm{s}}\left(\mu\right)\) can be written as \(\alpha_{\mathrm{s}}\left(\mu\right)=\frac{g_{\mathrm{s}}^{2}(\mu)}{4\pi} \approx\frac{4\pi}{\beta_{0}\ln\left(\mu^{2}/\Lambda^{2}\right)}\), where \(\Lambda\) is the QCD scale, \(\mu\) is the momentum transfer scale, and \(\beta_{0}\) is the first order beta-function which encodes the energy independent part of the coupling parameter. When \(\beta_{0}>0\) the coupling decreases logarithmically with energy, which is called asymptotic freedom.

To say this is simpler terms: the quarks become asymptotically less bound as energy in

