To study the effect of resonance decays on the analyses presented in this dissertation, one could identify the prominent resonances, then suppress the daughter particles by cutting out all pairs of particles of the correct species with \(q_{\rm inv}\)3 corresponding the mass of the mother particle. Correlations would be induced by the cut which would need to be corrected for with mixed events. This was not done in this analysis because no particle identification was performed.

Footnote 3: The \(\chi^{2}\) and \(\chi^{2}\) decay modes are not included in the analysis.

#### Changing Chemistry

Variations in the chemistry of events (the ratios of various particle species) could have an effect on the observed non-statistical fluctuations of \(\langle p_{\rm t}\rangle\). In the case of a perfectly thermal QGP system, different particle species will have different momenta because of their masses. Particle ratios are determined by the collision energy and temperature of the system, but will have statistical variation. Even in the case of constant temperature, variations in particle ratios will result in additional fluctuations of \(\langle p_{\rm t}\rangle\).

This effect could be investigated by performing the analyses using identified particles. Performing the analysis on only one particle species at a time should mitigate the effects of changing chemistry. Doing \(p_{\rm t}\) analyses on identified particles has the additional complication that particle identification is done by cutting on \(p_{\rm t}\) and \(p\). Truncating the underlying \(p_{\rm t}\) distributions will affect the fluctuations of \(\langle p_{\rm t}\rangle\), this effect would be non-trivial and have to be investigated.

#### Temperature Fluctuations

As event-by-event analyses became practical, one of the original motivations for studying \(\langle p_{\rm t}\rangle\) fluctuations was to measure temperature fluctuations and the specific heat of the produced systems. The specific heat, \(C_{V}\), would provide insight into the equation of state of the system and could be sensitive to the order of the phase transition. In the case of a first order phase transition, the specific heat will be much larger than in a smooth crossover. In a smooth cross-over fluctuations in the energy of the system will result in a fluctuation in the temperature, but in a first order phase transition fluctuations in energy may go into the latent heat of the phase transition and the temperature may fluctuate much less.

The motivation for using \(\langle p_{\rm t}\rangle\) fluctuations to measure specific heat is as follows. In a thermal system the \(\langle p_{\rm t}\rangle\) of a system should be related to the temperature \(T\) by some function \(\langle p_{\rm t}\rangle=F\left(\langle T\rangle\right)\). Likewise \(\langle\langle p_{\rm t}\rangle\rangle=F\left(\langle T\rangle\right)\). Assuming the temperature fluctuations are Gaussian, arguments from the theory of error propagation give [40],

\[\sigma_{\langle p_{\rm t}\rangle,{\rm dynamic}}=\left|F^{\prime}\left(\langle T \rangle\right)\right|\sigma_{T,{\rm dynamic}}, \tag{4.6}\]

where \(F^{\prime}\left(\langle T\rangle\right)\) is the derivative of \(F\left(\langle T\rangle\right)\). We can then write,

\[\frac{\sigma_{\langle p_{\rm t}\rangle,{\rm dynamic}}}{\langle\langle p_{\rm t }\rangle\rangle}=\left|\frac{F^{\prime}\left(\langle T\rangle\right)}{F\left( \langle T\rangle\right)}\right|\frac{\sigma_{T,{\rm dynamic}}}{\langle T \rangle}. \tag{4.7}\]

Treating the system as an ideal gas, there are two limiting cases for the \(p_{\rm t}\) spectra, a nonrelativistic ideal gas where \(\langle\langle p_{\rm t}\rangle\rangle\propto\sqrt{\langle T\rangle}\) and an ultrarelativistic ideal gas where \(\langle\langle p_{\rm t}\rangle\rangle\propto\langle T\rangle\). In reality the system will be somewhere between these limiting cases. It then followsthat,

\[\frac{\sigma_{\langle p_{\text{t}}\rangle,\text{dynamic}}}{\langle \langle p_{\text{t}}\rangle\rangle}=\epsilon\frac{\sigma_{T,\text{dynamic}}}{ \langle T\rangle}, \tag{4.8}\]

where \(\epsilon\) is some value between 0.5 and 1. Noting that \(C_{V}=\left(\frac{\langle T\rangle}{\sigma_{T,\text{dynamic}}}\right)^{2}\), we can say,

\[\left(\frac{\sigma_{\langle p_{\text{t}}\rangle,\text{dynamic}}}{\langle \langle p_{\text{t}}\rangle\rangle}\right)^{2}=\frac{\epsilon^{2}}{C_{V}}. \tag{4.9}\]

It is interesting to note that there will be statistical fluctuations of the temperature. Fluctuations in the temperature will go like \(\frac{\sigma_{T,\text{stat}}}{\langle T\rangle}\approx\frac{1}{\sqrt{\langle N \rangle}}\). Where \(\langle N\rangle\) is the mean multiplicity of the events. A ballpark value of \(\langle N\rangle\approx 500\), which gives \(\frac{\sigma_{T,\text{stat}}}{\langle T\rangle}=0.045\). The statistical fluctuations of temperature come from the limited number of particles, which is also the source of the statistical fluctuations of \(\langle p_{\text{t}}\rangle\), so it is unnecessary to make an additional correction explicitly for statistical temperature fluctuations.

#### Correlation Length

One of the most exciting possible sources of dynamic fluctuations of \(\langle p_{\text{t}}\rangle\) is the QCD critical point (see Section 1.2.2).

In Ref. [41], M. Stephanov works out a simplified theory of pions coupling to a field \(\sigma\) of mass \(\tilde{m}\), with interactions given by the Lagrangian

\[\mathcal{L}_{\sigma\pi\pi}=2G\sigma\pi^{+}\pi^{-} \tag{4.10}\]with coupling \(G\). He calls this model the Linear Sigma Model. He defines a universal fluctuation correlator \(\left\langle\Delta n_{p}^{\alpha},\Delta n_{k}^{\beta}\right\rangle\) which is a two particle correlation between occupation numbers. The subscripts and superscripts denote that \(n_{p}^{\alpha}\) is the occupation number of a quantum state defined by property \(\alpha\) with value \(p\). The value of \(n_{p}^{\alpha}\) will vary event to event, and can be averaged over many events to \(\left\langle n_{p}^{\alpha}\right\rangle\). Then \(\Delta n_{p}^{\alpha}=n_{p}^{\alpha}-\left\langle n_{p}^{\alpha}\right\rangle\). He demonstrates that the \(G^{2}\) order correction to the universal fluctuation correlator can be shown, to first order near the critical point to be,

\[\left\langle\Delta n_{p}^{\alpha},\Delta n_{k}^{\beta}\right\rangle_{G^{2}} \propto\frac{1}{\tilde{m}^{2}}. \tag{4.11}\]

This is notable because \(\tilde{m}\) is related to the correlation length, \(\xi=\tilde{m}^{-1}\) (see also Ref. [42]). Near the critical point, when \(\xi\) diverges, the universal fluctuation correlator should also diverge.

In Ref. [41] it is shown that the variance of \(\left\langle p_{\rm t}\right\rangle\) is related to \(\left\langle\Delta n_{i},\Delta n_{j}\right\rangle\) (the absence of superscripts is because the occupation numbers are implied to be of momentum states) by,

\[\sigma_{\left\langle p_{\rm t}\right\rangle}^{2}=\frac{1}{\left\langle N \right\rangle^{2}}\sum_{i}\sum_{j}\left\langle\Delta n_{i},\Delta n_{j} \right\rangle\left(p_{\rm t,i}-\left\langle p_{\rm t}\right\rangle\right) \left(p_{\rm t,j}-\left\langle p_{\rm t}\right\rangle\right). \tag{4.12}\]

However, the theoretical derivation assumes that there are no statistical fluctuations,

\(\sigma_{\left\langle p_{\rm t}\right\rangle,{\rm statistical}}^{2}= \left\langle\frac{\sigma_{p_{\rm t}}^{2}}{N}\right\rangle=0\) and so \(\sigma_{\left\langle p_{\rm t}\right\rangle}^{2}=\sigma_{\left\langle p_{\rm t }\right\rangle,{\rm dynamic}}^{2}\) (from Eq. 4.2). The physical quantity of interest is then \(\sigma_{\left\langle p_{\rm t}\right\rangle,{\rm dynamic}}^{2}\) and not \(\sigma_{\left\langle p_{\rm t}\right\rangle}^{2}\).

In another paper on the Linear Sigma Model, Stephanov also relates the third moment of the \(\left\langle p_{\text{t}}\right\rangle\) spectra to a generalized three particle correlator [42]\(\left\langle\Delta n_{i},\Delta n_{j},\Delta n_{k}\right\rangle\) by,

\[\left\langle\left(\Delta\left\langle p_{\text{t}}\right\rangle\right)^{3} \right\rangle=\frac{1}{\left\langle N\right\rangle^{3}}\sum_{i}\sum_{j}\sum_{k} \left\langle\Delta n_{i},\Delta n_{j},\Delta n_{k}\right\rangle\left(p_{\text{t },i}-\left\langle p_{\text{t}}\right\rangle\right)\left(p_{\text{t},j}-\left \langle p_{\text{t}}\right\rangle\right)\left(p_{\text{t},k}-\left\langle p_{ \text{t}}\right\rangle\right). \tag{4.13}\]

The three particle correlator is related to the correlation length by \(\left\langle\Delta n_{i},\Delta n_{j},\Delta n_{k}\right\rangle\propto\xi^{6}\). As with the two particle correlator, the _physical_ quantity of interest will actually be \(\left\langle\left(\Delta\left\langle p_{\text{t}}\right\rangle\right)^{3} \right\rangle_{\text{dynamic}}\) and not \(\left\langle\left(\Delta\left\langle p_{\text{t}}\right\rangle\right)^{3}\right\rangle\) because of non-physical statistical fluctuations. These general multi-particle correlators are related to the moments of the zero-momentum mode of sigma field, \(\sigma_{0}\equiv\int d^{4}\sigma\left(x\right)/V\). In Stephanov's Linear Sigma Model, the relationship between the moments of this sigma field and the correlation length is given by [42],

\[\kappa_{2} =\left\langle\sigma_{0}^{2}\right\rangle=\frac{T}{V}\xi^{2} \tag{4.14}\] \[\kappa_{3} =\left\langle\sigma_{0}^{3}\right\rangle=\frac{2\lambda_{3}T}{V} \xi^{6}\] \[\kappa_{4} =\left\langle\sigma_{0}^{4}\right\rangle_{c}=\left\langle\sigma_ {0}^{4}\right\rangle-\left\langle\sigma_{0}^{2}\right\rangle^{2}=\frac{6T}{V} \left[2\left(\lambda_{3}\xi\right)^{2}-\lambda_{4}\right]\xi^{8}\]

Like in Eqs. 4.12 and 4.13, we can relate the moments of the sigma field to dynamic moments of the \(\left\langle p_{\text{t}}\right\rangle\) distribution. However, we cannot expect the dynamic moments of the \(\left\langle p_{\text{t}}\right\rangle\) spectra to necessarily follow the fluctuations of the sigma field because there are many effects which can modify these fluctuations, and there are physical processes which will manifest in the dynamic correlations, but are not related to thermodynamics of the system. Also, the system has continued to interact for some time after passing near the critical point.

The critical contribution to the higher moments of the \(\left\langle p_{\text{t}}\right\rangle\) spectra have not been calculated, and will depend upon momentum distributions, quantum momentum states, momentum efficiencies, and potentially other factors. There are other analyses, outside of the scope of this dissertation which attempt to detect critical opalescence through the moments of the multiplicity distributions. For these analyses the relationship between the correlation length and the moments of the multiplicity distributions has been estimated to be [43, 44, 45],

\[\begin{split}&\kappa_{2}\propto\xi^{2},\\ &\kappa_{3}\propto\xi^{9/2},\\ &\kappa_{4}\propto\xi^{7},\\ & S\sigma\propto\xi^{5/2},\\ & K\sigma^{2}\propto\xi^{5},\end{split} \tag{4.15}\]

where \(S\) is the'skewness'4 and \(K\) is the kurtosis5. A similar relationship is expected for the higher moments of \(\langle p_{\text{t}}\rangle\).

Footnote 4: \(S=\frac{\mu_{3}}{\sigma^{3}}=\frac{\kappa_{3}}{\frac{3/2}{\kappa_{2}^{2}}}\)

Footnote 5: \(K=\frac{\mu_{4}}{\sigma^{4}}-3=\frac{\kappa_{4}}{\frac{3}{\kappa_{2}^{2}}}\)

7 Two-Particle \(p_{\text{t}}\) Correlations, \(\langle\Delta p_{\text{t},i},\Delta p_{\text{t},j}\rangle\)

The correlation function which is used in this dissertation is the two-particle relative transverse momentum correlator, \(\left\langle\Delta p_{\text{t},i},\Delta p_{\text{t},j}\right\rangle\). This correlator is equivalent the dynamic component of the variance of \(\langle p_{\text{t}}\rangle\), \(\sigma^{2}_{\langle p_{\text{t}}\rangle,\text{dynamic}}\). By construction it will be 0 for mixed events and statistically sampled events. The mathematical definition of this correlator is given in Section 4.7.1. It is also equivalent to the difference of the variance of the \(\langle p_{\text{t}}\rangle\) distribution and the average variance of the event-by-event \(p_{\text{t}}\) distribution scaled by average multiplicity.

This can be inferred from Eq. 4.2, but is also rigorously demonstrated in Appendix. The experimental analysis is presented in Chapters 5 and 6.

#### Mathematical Construction

The two-particle transverse momentum correlation observable, \(\left\langle\Delta p_{\text{t},i},\Delta p_{\text{t},j}\right\rangle\), is constructed as follows. For each event an average \(p_{\text{t}}\) is calculated by averaging the \(p_{\text{t}}\) values of each track within the analysis acceptance:

\[\left\langle p_{\text{t}}\right\rangle_{k}=\frac{\sum_{i=1}^{N_{k}}p_{\text{t}, i}}{N_{k}} \tag{4.16}\]

The values of \(\left\langle p_{\text{t}}\right\rangle_{k}\) are then averaged over multiple events. This event averaged \(p_{\text{t}}\) is done as a function of multiplicity (refMult) because the event average \(p_{\text{t}}\) is multiplicity dependent as has already been observed in other analyses [46].

\[\left\langle\left\langle p_{\text{t}}\right\rangle\right\rangle=\frac{\sum_{k= 1}^{N_{\text{event}}}\left\langle p_{\text{t}}\right\rangle_{k}}{N_{\text{ event}}} \tag{4.17}\]

At high multiplicities the number of events available decreases to zero, and the calculated value of \(\left\langle\left\langle p_{\text{t}}\right\rangle\right\rangle\) fluctuates due to limited statistics. To compensate for this statistical variation, \(\left\langle\left\langle p_{\text{t}}\right\rangle\right\rangle\) as a function of multiplicity is fit with a linear form at large multiplicites and the fitted values are used in further steps of the calculation. Examples of this fitting can be seen in Figures 4.3 and 4.4.

For each event the correlator is defined as a double sum, which is also equivalent to a single sum over all pairs of particles. The sum is taken over the product of the deviation of \(p_{\text{t}}\) for Figure 4.3: \(\langle\langle p_{\rm t}\rangle\rangle\) (refMult) for 7.7 GeV. The raw distribution is plotted in blue points, and the smoothed \(\langle\langle p_{\rm t}\rangle\rangle\) values in green. The error bands are statistical errors. The errors above refMult \(\approx 250\) grow very large due to low statistics.

Figure 4.4: \(\langle\langle p_{\rm t}\rangle\rangle\) (refMult) for 200 GeV. The raw distribution is plotted in blue points, and the smoothed \(\langle\langle p_{\rm t}\rangle\rangle\) values in green. The error bands are statistical errors. The errors above refMult \(\approx 600\) grow very large due to low statistics.

all pairs of particles in each event.

\[C_{k}=\sum_{i=1}^{N_{k}}\sum_{j=1,j\neq i}^{N_{k}}\left(p_{\text{t},i}-\left\langle \left\langle p_{\text{t}}\right\rangle\right\rangle\right)\left(p_{\text{t},j}- \left\langle\left\langle p_{\text{t}}\right\rangle\right\rangle\right) \tag{4.18}\]

The correlator is divided by the total number of pairs of particles in the event, and then averaged over all events.

\[\left\langle\Delta p_{\text{t},i},\Delta p_{\text{t},j}\right\rangle=\frac{1}{ N_{\text{events}}}\sum_{k=1}^{N_{\text{events}}}\frac{C_{k}}{N_{k}\left(N_{k}-1\right)} \tag{4.19}\]

Just as with \(\left\langle\left\langle p_{\text{t}}\right\rangle\right\rangle\), this average is done as a function of reference multiplicity. The values of \(\left\langle\Delta p_{\text{t},i},\Delta p_{\text{t},j}\right\rangle\) are averaged over each centrality bin (weighted by the number of events in each multiplicity bin).

#### Scalings

The purpose of the two-particle correlator is to isolate critical behavior. However, the two-particle correlator is also sensitive to physical effects not related to critical behavior. In order to remove these effects, and isolate critical behavior, various scalings are used. Scalings can also be used to attempt to equate results from different systems, for example: p+p, Cu+Cu, and Au+Au. If correlations between different systems could be shown to be equivalent using some scaling related to system parameters, it may be indicative of some underlying behavior which is constant between the differing systems.

#### 4.7.2.1 By \(\langle\langle p_{\rm t}\rangle\rangle^{-1}\)

The correlation observable may have an dependence arising from the energy dependence of \(\langle\langle p_{\rm t}\rangle\rangle\), so scaling with \(\langle\langle p_{\rm t}\rangle\rangle\) should in principle remove it. The correlation observable \(\left\langle\Delta p_{\rm t,i},\Delta p_{\rm t,j}\right\rangle\) has units of momentum squared (\(\left({\rm GeV/}c\right)^{2}\)), while \(\langle\langle p_{\rm t}\rangle\rangle\) has units of momentum (\({\rm GeV/}c\)), so when scaling with \(\langle\langle p_{\rm t}\rangle\rangle\) we must either square \(\langle\langle p_{\rm t}\rangle\rangle\) or take the square root of the correlation observable. In addition to addressing possible energy dependence, this scaling provides two other benefits, first when scaled as \(\sqrt{\left\langle\Delta p_{\rm t,i},\Delta p_{\rm t,j}\right\rangle}/\left\langle \langle p_{\rm t}\rangle\right\rangle\) our observable is equivalent to the CERES observable \(\Sigma_{p_{\rm t}}\)[36]. Second, the efficiency dependence of \(\sqrt{\left\langle\Delta p_{\rm t,i},\Delta p_{\rm t,j}\right\rangle}\) is the same as that of \(\langle\langle p_{\rm t}\rangle\rangle\) (see Section 6.2) so the scaled observable is independent of efficiency.

The strongest motivation for so scaling with \(\langle\langle p_{\rm t}\rangle\rangle\) comes from Section 4.6.5. Noting that \(\left\langle\Delta p_{\rm t,i},\Delta p_{\rm t,j}\right\rangle=\sigma_{\langle p _{\rm t}\rangle,{\rm dynamic}}^{2}\), from Equations 4.8 and 4.9 we can write,

\[\frac{\sqrt{\left\langle\Delta p_{\rm t,i},\Delta p_{\rm t,j}\right\rangle}}{ \langle\langle p_{\rm t}\rangle\rangle}=\epsilon\frac{\sigma_{T,{\rm dynamic }}}{\langle T\rangle}=\frac{\epsilon}{\sqrt{C_{V}}}, \tag{4.20}\]

where, as in Equations 4.8 and 4.9, \(\epsilon\) is some number between 0.5 and 1 coming from the relationship between \(\langle\langle p_{\rm t}\rangle\rangle\) and \(\langle T\rangle\), and \(C_{V}\) is the specific heat of the system.

#### 4.7.2.2 By Multiplicity

There are two independent motivations for scaling with variants of multiplicity. The first motivation is to explore the possibility that heavy-ion collisions behave like a large number of nucleon-nucleon collisions. If that were the case, then one would expect the correlations to scale with the number of individual nucleon-nucleon collisions (\(N_{\rm coll}\)). The value of \(N_{\rm coll}\) isnot experimentally accessible, but could be estimated using Glauber simulations; it should, in principle, be related to to the collision energy and the detected multiplicity. The \(N_{\rm coll}\) scaling is not presented in this dissertation.

The other motivation for scaling with multiplicity comes from the fact that \(\left\langle\Delta p_{\rm t,\it i},\Delta p_{\rm t,\it j}\right\rangle\) is calculated as an average over all pairs. As the number of particles in an event (\(N\)) increases, the number of pairs increases like \(N^{2}\), so the summation 'dilutes' the correlations. This dilution goes as \(1/N\), so it can be addressed by scaling with the number of particles used in the double summation.

##### 4.7.2.3 By \(\left\langle\left\langle p_{\rm t}\right\rangle\right\rangle^{-1}\) and Multiplicity

The arguments presented for each scaling are both valid, so in addition to presenting them individually, results will be presented of the correlation observable scaled by both \(\left\langle\left\langle p_{\rm t}\right\rangle\right\rangle^{-1}\) and \(N\).

### 4.8 Higher Moments of \(\left\langle p_{\rm t}\right\rangle\)

The moments of distributions corresponding to fluctuation observables are potential measures of critical behavior because they relate to the correlation length of the medium. The mathematical description of the higher moments is presented in Section 4.8.1. Baseline expectations of statistical behavior are presented in Section 4.8.2. The corresponding experimental analysis is presented in Chapters 7 and 8.

#### Mathematical Construction

All of the moments of the \(\langle p_{\text{t}}\rangle\) distributions have the same form. The moment of order \(n\) is defined as,

\[\mu_{n}=\frac{\sum_{i=1}^{N_{\text{events}}}{(\langle p_{\text{t}}\rangle_{i}- \langle\langle p_{\text{t}}\rangle))}^{n}}{N_{\text{events}}}. \tag{4.21}\]

The first moment is more commonly known as the mean, and the second moment as the variance. The moments of the distribution are also related to the cumulants of the distribution. The first six cumulants are related to the moments by,

\[\begin{split}\kappa_{1}&=\mu_{1},\\ \kappa_{2}&=\mu_{2},\\ \kappa_{3}&=\mu_{3},\\ \kappa_{4}&=\mu_{4}-3\mu_{2}^{2},\\ \kappa_{5}&=\mu_{5}-10\mu_{2}\mu_{3},\\ \kappa_{6}&=\mu_{6}-15\mu_{2}\mu_{4}-10\mu_{3}^{2}+ 30\mu_{2}^{3}.\end{split} \tag{4.22}\]

In addition to being expressed as cumulants, the higher moments are sometimes expressed as classical named properties of distributions: variance (\(\sigma^{2}\)), skewness (\(S\)), and kurtosis (\(K\)), which are related to the moments and cumulants by,\[\sigma^{2}=\mu_{2}=\kappa_{2},\] \[S=\frac{\mu_{3}}{\sigma^{3}}=\frac{\kappa_{3}}{\kappa_{2}^{3/2}}, \tag{4.23}\] \[K=\frac{\mu_{4}}{\sigma^{4}}-3=\frac{\kappa_{4}}{\kappa_{2}^{2}}.\]

These are also expressed as moments products, which can be written in terms of ratios of the cumulants,

\[\begin{split}& S\sigma=\frac{\kappa_{3}}{\kappa_{2}},\\ & K\sigma^{2}=\frac{\kappa_{4}}{\kappa_{2}}.\end{split} \tag{4.24}\]

Each moment and cumulant is calculated individually for each multiplicity bin. This is done so that the variation in the value of \(\langle\langle p_{\text{t}}\rangle\rangle\) with multiplicity does not skew the other moments. After all of the calculations are done for each multiplicity, the centrality bin values are calculated by taking an average over all of the multiplicity bins in each centrality bin, weighting each multiplicity bin by the number of events in that bin.

Unlike with the two-particle correlator analysis, the value of \(\langle\langle p_{\text{t}}\rangle\rangle\) is not smoothed at large multiplicity values with a linear fit.

