## Appendix A

## Appendix A Derivation of

Here we will break down the two-particle transverse momentum (\(p_{\text{t}}\)) correlation observable, \(\left\langle\Delta p_{\text{t},i},\Delta p_{\text{t},j}\right\rangle\), which has been used in several STAR analyses [36, 39]. We will demonstrate that \(\left\langle\Delta p_{\text{t},i},\Delta p_{\text{t},j}\right\rangle\) can be written as a sum of two parts, one corresponding to the average covariance of the average \(p_{\text{t}}\) spectrum, and another corresponding to the second moment of the \(\left\langle p_{\text{t}}\right\rangle\) spectra and the average number of tracks used in the calculation.

### A.1 Additional Notation

The correlation observable \(\left\langle\Delta p_{\text{t},i},\Delta p_{\text{t},j}\right\rangle\) is given in Section 4.7.1. Before deriving our result we will need to define a few more terms. First, without too much concern as to the physical significance, we define a transverse momentum sample self-covariance for an event \(k\),

\[\sigma_{k,k}^{2}=\frac{\sum_{i=1}^{N_{k}}\sum_{j=1}^{N_{k}}\left(p_{\text{t},i }-\left\langle p_{\text{t}}\right\rangle_{k}\right)\left(p_{\text{t},j}- \left\langle p_{\text{t}}\right\rangle_{k}\right)}{N_{k}^{2}}.\] (A.1)

The self-covariance \(\sigma_{k,k}^{2}\) is always zero. The only assumption necessary to demonstrate thisis that the underlying distribution is bound. In the experimental case, the underlying \(p_{\text{t}}\) distributions are bound to a range \(\left[p_{\text{t,min}},p_{\text{t,max}}\right]\) by analysis cuts, but even in the ideal case all that is needed is that the the underlying distribution is positive and integrable.

First we write Eq. A.1 expanding the \(\left\langle p_{\text{t}}\right\rangle\) terms,

\[\sigma_{k,k}^{2}=\frac{\sum_{i=1}^{N}\sum_{j=1}^{N}\left(p_{\text{t,}i}-\frac{ \sum_{k}^{N}p_{\text{t,}k}}{N}\right)\left(p_{\text{t,}j}-\frac{\sum_{k}^{N}p _{\text{t,}k}}{N}\right)}{N^{2}}.\] (A.2)

We can then multiply and regroup,

\[\sigma_{k,k}^{2}=\frac{\sum_{i=1}^{N}\sum_{j=1}^{N}\left[p_{\text{t,}i}p_{ \text{t,}j}-\frac{\sum_{k}^{N}p_{\text{t,}k}}{N}\left(p_{\text{t,}i}+p_{\text {t,}j}\right)+\left(\frac{\sum_{k}^{N}p_{\text{t,}k}}{N}\right)^{2}\right]}{N^ {2}}.\] (A.3)

Performing the summation on the last two terms in the numerator and simplifying,

\[\sigma_{k,k}^{2}=\frac{\sum_{i=1}^{N}\sum_{j=1}^{N}p_{\text{t,}i}p_{\text{t, }j}-\left(\sum_{k}^{N}p_{\text{t,}k}\right)^{2}}{N^{2}}=\frac{\sum_{i=1}^{N} \sum_{j=1}^{N}p_{\text{t,}i}p_{\text{t,}j}-N^{2}\left\langle p_{\text{t}} \right\rangle^{2}}{N^{2}}.\] (A.4)

The final step is to note that \(\sum_{i=1}^{N}\sum_{j=1}^{N}p_{\text{t,}i}p_{\text{t,}j}=N\left\langle p_{ \text{t}}\right\rangle\sum_{j=1}^{N}p_{\text{t,}i}=N^{2}\left\langle p_{\text {t}}\right\rangle^{2}\). This may not be immediately obvious, but it arrises from the fact that a one dimensional distribution _cannot_ be correlated with itself, and the mean of the product of two uncorrelated distributions is the product of the means. Stated mathematically, \(\left\langle xy\right\rangle=\left\langle x\right\rangle\times\left\langle y\right\rangle\). Therefore, \(\sigma_{k,k}^{2}=0\).

We also define an "idealized" variant of \(C_{k}\) (Eq. 4.18),\[\left\{C_{k}\right\}=\sum_{i=1}^{N_{k}}\sum_{j=1,j\neq i}^{N_{k}}\left(p_{{\rm t}, i}-\left\langle p_{\rm t}\right\rangle_{k}\right)\left(p_{{\rm t},j}-\left\langle p _{\rm t}\right\rangle_{k}\right).\] (A.5)

\(\left\{C_{k}\right\}\) is "idealized" in that \(\left\{C_{k}\right\}=C_{k}\) if all events have the same \(\left\langle p_{\rm t}\right\rangle\).

Note that Eq. A.1 and Eq. A.5 can be related by,

\[\left\{C_{k}\right\}=N_{k}\left(N_{k}-1\right)\sigma_{k,k}-\sum_{i=1}^{N_{k}} \left(p_{{\rm t},i}-\left\langle p_{\rm t}\right\rangle_{k}\right)^{2}.\] (A.6)

Noting that \(\sigma_{k,k}^{2}=0\) and that the sample variance \(\sigma_{p_{\rm t},k}^{2}\) is defined \(\sigma_{x,k}^{2}=\frac{\sum_{i=1}^{N_{k}}\left(x_{i}-\left\langle x\right\rangle _{k}\right)}{N_{k}}\) we can simplify further to,

\[\left\{C_{k}\right\}=-N_{k}\sigma_{p_{\rm t},k}^{2}.\] (A.7)

Lastly, before returning to \(\left\langle\Delta p_{{\rm t},i},\Delta p_{{\rm t},j}\right\rangle\), we define for each event \(k\),

\[\Delta\left\langle p_{\rm t}\right\rangle_{k}=\left\langle p_{\rm t}\right \rangle_{k}-\left\langle\left\langle p_{\rm t}\right\rangle\right\rangle,\] (A.8)

which is the deviation of the mean transverse momentum with respect to the mean over many events.

