We can then simplify Eq. A.11 to,

\[\left\langle\Delta p_{\text{t},i},\Delta p_{\text{t},j}\right\rangle=\frac{1}{N _{\text{events}}}\sum_{k=1}^{N_{\text{events}}}\left(\frac{\left\{C_{k} \right\}}{N_{k}\left(N_{k}-1\right)}+\left(\Delta\left\langle p_{\text{t}} \right\rangle_{k}\right)^{2}\right).\] (A.12)

Using Eq. A.7, we can replace \(\frac{\left\{C_{k}\right\}}{N_{k}(N_{k}-1)}\),

\[\left\langle\Delta p_{\text{t},i},\Delta p_{\text{t},j}\right\rangle=\frac{1}{ N_{\text{events}}}\sum_{k=1}^{N_{\text{events}}}\left(-\frac{\sigma_{p_{\text{t} },k}^{2}}{N_{k}-1}+\left(\Delta\left\langle p_{\text{t}}\right\rangle_{k} \right)^{2}\right).\] (A.13)

Performing the average we get,

\[\left\langle\Delta p_{\text{t},i},\Delta p_{\text{t},j}\right\rangle=\left\langle \left(\Delta\left\langle p_{\text{t}}\right\rangle\right)^{2}\right\rangle- \left\langle\frac{\sigma_{p_{\text{t}}}^{2}}{N-1}\right\rangle.\] (A.14)

Note that the first term, \(\left\langle\left(\Delta\left\langle p_{\text{t}}\right\rangle\right)^{2}\right\rangle\), is \(\kappa_{2}^{\left\langle p_{\text{t}}\right\rangle}\), the second moment of the \(\left\langle p_{\text{t}}\right\rangle\) distribution, or equivalently \(\sigma_{\left\langle p_{\text{t}}\right\rangle}^{2}\). We can also replace \(\left(N-1\right)\) with \(N\) because they will be almost identical for large values.

\[\left\langle\Delta p_{\text{t},i},\Delta p_{\text{t},j}\right\rangle=\sigma_{ \left\langle p_{\text{t}}\right\rangle}^{2}-\left\langle\frac{\sigma_{p_{ \text{t}}}^{2}}{N}\right\rangle.\] (A.15)

So the two particle correlation observable \(\left\langle\Delta p_{\text{t},i},\Delta p_{\text{t},j}\right\rangle\) is equivalent to the second moment of the \(\left\langle p_{\text{t}}\right\rangle\) distribution minus the variance in the underlying event \(p_{\text{t}}\) distributions scaled by the number of tracks.

